# Responsive UI is required

Treat responsiveness as part of implementation, not follow-up polish.

## Build

- Start mobile-first and let layouts expand progressively.
- Prefer normal flow, Grid/Flexbox, wrapping, `minmax()`, relative units, and existing breakpoints.
- Avoid fixed widths and heights when content or viewport size can vary.
- Never require horizontal page scrolling at 320px. Intrinsically wide controls may scroll inside a clearly bounded region.
- Keep actions, labels, validation, dialogs, and navigation usable at every supported size.
- Account for long names, browser zoom, safe areas, and mobile viewport height.
- Keep interactive targets comfortably touchable, normally at least 44 by 44 CSS pixels.
- Do not create separate mobile and desktop component trees unless their behavior genuinely differs.

## Verify

- Exercise changed UI at representative phone (320–390px), tablet (~768px), desktop (~1280px), and wide-screen widths.
- Check portrait and landscape when layout or viewport height matters.
- Confirm there is no clipping, overlap, unintended overflow, unreadably narrow content, or functionality available at only one breakpoint.
- Add the smallest useful Playwright coverage when responsive behavior is non-trivial or has regressed before.
- If responsive verification cannot be run, say so instead of claiming it passed.
